{
  "note": "Shared truth for every pane layout in the four kits: the AppLayout family (AppLayout, DesktopAppShell, IMAppKit, WorkspaceFrame) and the conversation family (ResponsiveLayout, ConversationWorkspace). All four platforms consume this file. `panes` is the one rule for how many panes fit side by side (FR-110). With chat = chatMinWidth (360) x max(1, textScale): two panes need navigationWidth + primaryWidth + chat; three need that plus detailWidth. Defaults: navigationWidth 0, primaryWidth 320 (primaryPaneDefaultWidth), detailWidth 300 (detailPaneDefaultWidth). Only the chat grows with the text: navigation, list and detail are drawn at fixed widths on every kit, so asking for them at a scaled width reserves room nobody draws, and the chat is not capped, because a cap hands the readers who need the room most a chat below its own minimum. Hairline separators are not counted: at exactly a threshold a separator takes one pixel from the chat. There is no device floor: the 720 and 1100 floors this rule replaced had no content behind them (a list and a usable chat need 680). `cases` is the workspace presentation on top of it. mode = resolveApplicationResponsiveMode(width, textScale): <600 mobile, <900 tablet, <1500 desktop, else wideDesktop, on width/textScale — that is which navigation to draw, a different question from how many panes fit. navigationWidth by mode: tablet rail 72, desktop sidebar 280 (primaryPaneMinWidth), wideDesktop expanded sidebar 320 (primaryPaneDefaultWidth), 0 when the shell has no navigation (`navigation: false`). mobile is one pane and a detail is a route. tablet takes the panes rule without a third pane (its layout has no detail column): one pane makes the detail a route, two make it an overlay. desktop and wideDesktop take the panes rule as it is: three panes put the detail inline, two make it an overlay, one makes it a route. A width the caller does not know keeps the width-blind answer: two panes on a tablet, and on a desktop three with a detail or two without.",
